  "question": {
    "text": "Let $p(x)$ be a function defined on $R$ such that $p^{\\prime}(x)=p^{\\prime}(1-x)$, for all $x \\in[0,1], p(0)=1$ and $p(1)=41$. Then $\\int_0^1 p(x) d x$ equals"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "\n21\n",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "\n41\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n42\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$\\sqrt{41}$",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\mathrm{p}^{\\prime}(\\mathrm{x})=\\mathrm{p}^{\\prime}(1-\\mathrm{x})$\n$\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{p}(\\mathrm{x})=-\\mathrm{p}(1-\\mathrm{x})+\\mathrm{c}$\nat $x=0$\n$\\mathrm{p}(0)=-\\mathrm{p}(1)+\\mathrm{c} \\quad \\Rightarrow 42=\\mathrm{c}$\nnow $\\mathrm{p}(\\mathrm{x})=-\\mathrm{p}(1-\\mathrm{x})+42$\n$\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{p}(\\mathrm{x})+\\mathrm{p}(1-\\mathrm{x})=42$\n$\\mathrm{I}=\\int_0^1 \\mathrm{p}(\\mathrm{x}) \\mathrm{dx}=\\int_0^1 \\mathrm{p}(1-\\mathrm{x}) \\mathrm{dx}$\n$2 \\mathrm{I}=\\int_0^1(42) \\mathrm{dx} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{I}=21$"
  }
}